<p>
Years ago when I first starting using Emacs seriously, one of my go to sites for inspiration and ideas was Dirk-Jan C. Binnema&rsquo;s <a href="https://emacs-fu.blogspot.com">EMACS-FU</a>. He always had good ideas, not the least of which is the wonderful email client mu4e that I and many others are still using today. Thirteen years ago, Binnema stopped blogging although his site was still active. Despite the fact that he was not blogging he <i>was</i> working on mu/mu4e and other projects.
</p>
<p>
Now, after all those years, he&rsquo;s published <a href="https://emacs-fu.blogspot.com/2026/10/mu-1114.html">another post on EMACS-FU</a>. This times it&rsquo;s about his just released new version (1.14.4) of mu4e. Most of the post is concerned with the mu4e changes in the latest release. A (very) broad summary of these changes are:
</p>
<ul class="org-ul">
<li>Some enhancements in support of the Inbox Zero concept</li>
<li>Support for MIME type icons</li>
<li>Completion</li>
<li>Language detection</li>
<li>Attachment in replies</li>
<li>Enhanced support for HTML messages</li>
<li>Enhanced desktop notifications</li>
</ul>
<p>
By far the most exciting news for me is about Binnema&rsquo;s work on xwidgets. As most of you probably know, Emacs&rsquo; support of xwidgets has been broken for a while because of changes to the xwidgets library that removed a feature that Emacs depended on. Binnema has fixed that so now (as of Emacs 31.1) Emacs can support up-to-date xwidgets libraries again. That&rsquo;s a real boon for me because I use xwidgets for both email in mu4e and for Elfeed.
</p>
<p>
In any event, I&rsquo;m thrilled to see Binnema&rsquo;s return even if it amounts to no more than a visit. I do hope, however, that I won&rsquo;t have to wait 13 years for his next post.</p>
